{"name":"Savannah Grass","hex":"#babc72","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"savannah","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of savanna."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/savannah"},{"term":"grass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the family Poaceae, characterized by leaves that arise from nodes in the stem and leaf bases that wrap around the stem, especially those grown as ground cover rather than for grain.","Any of the various plants that are not in the family Poaceae that resemble grasses."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lay out on the grass; to knock down (an opponent etc.).","To act as a grass or informer, to betray; to report on (criminals etc) to the authorities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Savanna","blocks":[{"text":"A savanna or savannah is a mixed woodland-grassland biome and ecosystem characterised by the trees being sufficiently widely spaced so that the canopy does not close. The open canopy allows sufficient light to reach the ground to support an unbroken herbaceous layer consisting primarily of grasses. Four savanna forms exist; savanna woodland, where trees and shrubs form a light canopy; tree savanna, with scattered trees and shrubs; shrub savanna, with distributed shrubs; and grass savanna, where trees and shrubs are mostly nonexistent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Tarangire-Natpark800600.jpg/330px-Tarangire-Natpark800600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savanna","wikidata":"Q42320"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg/500px-Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg/1280px-Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg/500px-Savanna_Grasslands_%28199168845%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A grass savanna at Kruger National Park in South Africa","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASavanna_Grasslands_(199168845).jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Australian_savanna.jpg/330px-Australian_savanna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Australian_savanna.jpg/330px-Australian_savanna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A savanna woodland in Northern Australia demonstrating the regular tree spacing characteristic of some savannas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_savanna.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Bushfire_Australia.jpg/500px-Bushfire_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Bushfire_Australia.jpg/1280px-Bushfire_Australia.jpg?utm_source